e a Democratic hold at 95 to 98 percent", "Open seat after McKee lost renomination, with Ken Block's independent bid adding mild uncertainty"], "rationale": "Rhode Island's Democratic lean is reinforced by unanimous Solid/Safe D ratings, a stable polling lead for Foulkes over Guckian in the low-to-mid 20s, and markets pricing a Democratic win above 95 percent. The open seat and a credible independent campaign warrant some caution but do not change the expected outcome.", "watch": "Whether Ken Block consolidates anti-Foulkes sentiment after the final debates enough to compress the margin, even if the winner is unlikely to change.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Dan McKee (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Rhode Island's governorship is open after Dan McKee lost the Democratic nomination, and Helena Foulkes enters the general election as a clear favorite over Republican Aaron Guckian and a field of independents led by Ken Block. Every major race rating, from the Cook Political Report to Sabato's Crystal Ball, places the contest in Solid or Safe Democratic territory, and prediction markets give Democrats roughly a 95 to 98 percent chance of holding the office.\n\nPolling has been steady for months: surveys of likely voters show Foulkes leading Guckian by roughly 20 to 30 points, well beyond the state's eight-point Democratic lean. Block runs a visible campaign and stays closer to Foulkes in some head-to-head matchups, but the Democratic-Republican gap remains wide. The realistic question is the size of Foulkes's victory margin, not which party wins.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.996, "margin": 18.1}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.997, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9964, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9941, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9953, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9924, "margin": 19.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9959, "margin": 1